window.AWS_BEGINNER_COURSE = [{"title":"Start here: Cloud and AWS","subtitle":"Understand the big picture before service names.","lessons":[{"title":"What is cloud computing?","duration":6,"lead":"Cloud computing means renting computing resources over the internet instead of buying and maintaining all the hardware yourself.","plain":"Imagine you need electricity. You normally use the power grid instead of building your own power station. AWS works in a similar way for servers, storage, databases, and networking: you use what you need and AWS operates the underlying facilities.","analogy":"AWS is like a large technology utility company. You choose the services, capacity, and settings; AWS runs the physical data centers and hardware.","flow":["You need computing","Choose an AWS service","AWS provides capacity","You pay for usage"],"points":["A server is a computer that runs applications for other users.","Cloud resources can be created and removed quickly.","You still design, configure, secure, and monitor what you build.","Cloud does not automatically make an application secure or inexpensive."],"example":"A small shop can place its website on AWS today without buying a server, waiting for delivery, or maintaining a server room.","terms":[["On premises","Computers and equipment owned or operated in your own building or data center."],["Cloud","Computing resources delivered as services over a network."],["Pay as you go","Charges based mostly on the resources and time you use."]],"check":{"q":"Why might a small company choose AWS instead of buying a server?","options":["It can create resources quickly without owning hardware","AWS writes the entire application automatically","Cloud resources never need security settings","Every AWS service is free"],"correct":0,"explain":"The main advantage here is fast access to managed infrastructure without buying and operating physical hardware."}},{"title":"Regions and Availability Zones","duration":7,"lead":"AWS has data centers around the world. You choose where your resources run by selecting a Region.","plain":"A Region is a geographic area such as Singapore or Frankfurt. Each Region contains multiple isolated groups of data centers called Availability Zones, usually shortened to AZs.","analogy":"Think of a Region as a city and Availability Zones as separate buildings in that city. If one building has a problem, another building can keep your service running.","flow":["Choose a Region","Use two or more AZs","Place copies in different AZs","Survive one AZ failure"],"points":["Most AWS resources belong to one Region.","An Availability Zone is isolated from other AZs but connected with fast networking.","Using multiple AZs improves availability inside a Region.","Using multiple Regions is a larger design decision for global users or disaster recovery."],"example":"A website can run application servers in two Availability Zones behind one load balancer. If one AZ fails, traffic can continue to the other AZ.","terms":[["Region","A geographic AWS area containing multiple Availability Zones."],["Availability Zone","An isolated location within a Region containing one or more data centers."],["High availability","A design that keeps a service working when a component fails."]],"check":{"q":"What is the simplest way to protect an application from one Availability Zone failing?","options":["Run everything on one larger server","Place application capacity in at least two AZs","Create more IAM users","Use a longer password"],"correct":1,"explain":"Running capacity across multiple AZs prevents one AZ from being the only place where the application can operate."}},{"title":"Your first AWS mental model","duration":6,"lead":"Most AWS applications combine a few basic jobs: receive traffic, run code, store files, and store data.","plain":"You do not need to memorize hundreds of services. First learn the job each service performs. Later you can compare services that perform similar jobs.","analogy":"A restaurant has a front door, waiters, a kitchen, storage rooms, and records. An AWS application has networking, compute, storage, and databases.","flow":["User request","Network entry","Compute runs code","Database or storage","Response to user"],"points":["Networking moves requests to the correct place.","Compute services run application code.","Storage services keep files or disks.","Database services organize application data.","Security and monitoring surround every part of the design."],"example":"A photo website might use Route 53 for its domain, CloudFront for delivery, an ALB and EC2 for the app, S3 for photos, and a database for user information.","terms":[["Compute","Resources that run program instructions."],["Storage","Resources that keep files, objects, or disk data."],["Database","A service that stores structured application information and supports queries."]],"check":{"q":"Which category normally runs application code?","options":["Compute","DNS only","Billing","A Region name"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compute services such as EC2 and Lambda execute application code."}}]},{"title":"How a web application works","subtitle":"Follow one user request from browser to database.","lessons":[{"title":"The journey of a request","duration":7,"lead":"When you open a website, several components cooperate before the page appears.","plain":"The browser first finds the website address, connects to an entry point, and sends a request. The application processes the request, may read data, then sends a response back.","analogy":"It is like sending a parcel: an address system finds the destination, a delivery network moves it, and the recipient processes it.","flow":["Browser","DNS finds address","Load balancer receives","Server runs code","Database returns data","Browser receives page"],"points":["DNS translates a name such as example.com into a destination.","A load balancer can distribute requests across multiple servers.","The application server handles business logic.","A database stores information such as users and orders."],"example":"A login request reaches a load balancer, is sent to an application server, and the server checks the user record in a database.","terms":[["DNS","The system that maps human-readable names to network destinations."],["Request","A message asking a service to perform an action or return data."],["Response","The result returned by a service."]],"check":{"q":"What usually translates a domain name into a network destination?","options":["DNS","An EBS volume","A password policy","A snapshot"],"correct":0,"explain":"DNS is the naming system that helps clients locate services."}},{"title":"Stateless and stateful components","duration":7,"lead":"A stateless component can handle the next request without remembering what happened on the previous server.","plain":"Stateless application servers are easier to replace and scale because any healthy server can process a request. Important state should live in a shared database, cache, or storage service.","analogy":"A ticket counter is stateless when every worker can read the same central booking system. It is stateful if one worker keeps the only copy of your booking on paper.","flow":["Request arrives","Any server can handle it","Shared data store holds state","Server can be replaced"],"points":["User sessions stored only in one server's memory create a dependency on that server.","Shared state makes horizontal scaling easier.","Databases are normally stateful because their data must persist.","Stateless does not mean the whole application has no data."],"example":"Store shopping-cart data in DynamoDB or ElastiCache rather than only in one EC2 instance's memory.","terms":[["State","Information that must be remembered between operations."],["Stateless","A component that does not depend on local memory from an earlier request."],["Horizontal scaling","Adding more servers or workers instead of only making one server larger."]],"check":{"q":"Why are stateless application servers easier to scale?","options":["Any healthy server can handle a request","They never use a database","They have unlimited memory","They do not need networking"],"correct":0,"explain":"When state is shared elsewhere, requests can be distributed across interchangeable servers."}},{"title":"The shared responsibility model","duration":7,"lead":"AWS secures the cloud infrastructure, while you secure how you use AWS.","plain":"AWS protects physical data centers, hardware, and the managed service infrastructure. You are responsible for identities, permissions, data, network settings, and many configuration choices.","analogy":"A landlord secures the building structure, but a tenant still locks the apartment, controls who gets keys, and protects personal belongings.","flow":["AWS secures facilities","AWS operates infrastructure","You configure services","You protect identities and data"],"points":["Your responsibility changes depending on the service.","With EC2, you manage the operating system and patches.","With a more managed service, AWS handles more of the underlying platform.","Misconfiguration is still your responsibility."],"example":"AWS operates S3, but you decide whether an S3 bucket is private, who can read it, and whether encryption or versioning is enabled.","terms":[["Shared responsibility","Security duties divided between AWS and the customer."],["Managed service","A service where AWS operates more of the underlying technology."],["Configuration","Settings that control how a resource behaves."]],"check":{"q":"Who is responsible for deciding which users may read your private S3 data?","options":["You, the AWS customer","The internet service provider","Every AWS customer together","Nobody"],"correct":0,"explain":"AWS provides permission controls, but the customer configures who may access the data."}}]},{"title":"Identity and permissions","subtitle":"Control who can do what in your AWS account.","lessons":[{"title":"IAM users, groups, and roles","duration":8,"lead":"AWS Identity and Access Management, or IAM, controls access to AWS resources.","plain":"An IAM user is a long-term identity, a group organizes users, and a role is an identity that can be assumed temporarily by people or AWS services.","analogy":"A user is an employee badge, a group is a department, and a role is a temporary job badge used for a specific task.","flow":["Identity requests action","IAM checks permissions","Allowed or denied","Action reaches resource"],"points":["Use the root user only for rare account-level tasks.","Human users should use strong authentication and preferably federation or IAM Identity Center.","AWS services such as EC2 should usually use roles instead of stored access keys.","Roles provide temporary credentials."],"example":"An EC2 instance that reads from S3 can receive an IAM role. The application does not need hard-coded access keys.","terms":[["IAM user","A long-term identity inside one AWS account."],["IAM group","A collection of IAM users used to assign permissions."],["IAM role","An assumable identity that supplies temporary permissions."]],"check":{"q":"What should an EC2 application normally use to access S3?","options":["An IAM role attached to the instance","The root user's password","Access keys written in source code","A public S3 bucket"],"correct":0,"explain":"An instance role supplies temporary credentials without storing long-lived keys in code."},"lab":"iam.html"},{"title":"Policies and least privilege","duration":8,"lead":"An IAM policy is a document that describes allowed or denied actions.","plain":"Policies are attached to identities or resources. Start with the minimum permissions required, then add permissions only when a real need is understood.","analogy":"A hotel key card opens only your room and shared areas—not every room in the building.","flow":["Policy statement","Action + resource","Optional conditions","Allow or deny decision"],"points":["A policy can specify actions, resources, and conditions.","An explicit Deny overrides an Allow.","No matching Allow normally means the request is denied.","Avoid broad permissions such as all actions on all resources unless truly required."],"example":"A backup process may need s3:PutObject only in one backup bucket, not full access to every S3 bucket.","terms":[["Policy","A JSON document containing permission statements."],["Least privilege","Granting only the access necessary for a task."],["Explicit deny","A deny statement that overrides matching allows."]],"check":{"q":"What happens when one matching policy allows an action but another matching policy explicitly denies it?","options":["The explicit deny wins","The allow always wins","AWS chooses randomly","The most recent policy wins"],"correct":0,"explain":"An explicit Deny has priority in AWS policy evaluation."},"lab":"iam.html"},{"title":"Safe account basics","duration":6,"lead":"A few habits prevent many AWS account problems.","plain":"Protect the root user, use multi-factor authentication, avoid sharing credentials, rotate or remove unused credentials, and separate permissions by task.","analogy":"Do not use the master key for everyday work. Keep it locked away and use normal keys for normal tasks.","flow":["Protect root","Use MFA","Use roles or federation","Review permissions","Remove unused access"],"points":["Enable MFA for important identities.","Do not share one user account among multiple people.","Do not commit access keys to GitHub.","Use CloudTrail to record many account actions.","Set billing alerts early."],"example":"Create an administrative identity for daily work, enable MFA, and leave the root user unused except for tasks that require it.","terms":[["MFA","A second authentication factor in addition to a password."],["Access key","A credential used by software to call AWS APIs."],["Federation","Using an external identity system to sign in to AWS."]],"check":{"q":"Which identity should be avoided for everyday work?","options":["The AWS account root user","A limited role","An application role","An IAM Identity Center user"],"correct":0,"explain":"The root user has powerful account-level access and should be reserved for rare tasks."}}]},{"title":"Compute: running code","subtitle":"Learn EC2, Auto Scaling, load balancing, and Lambda.","lessons":[{"title":"EC2 in plain English","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers that you configure and operate.","plain":"You choose an operating system image, instance size, network, storage, and security settings. EC2 is flexible, but you manage more than with serverless or fully managed services.","analogy":"EC2 is like renting an unfurnished apartment: the building exists, but you choose and maintain much of what is inside.","flow":["Choose AMI","Choose instance type","Attach storage","Place in VPC","Start server"],"points":["An AMI is a template for the operating system and initial software.","An instance type defines CPU, memory, networking, and other capabilities.","Security groups control network traffic to and from the instance.","EBS commonly provides persistent block storage."],"example":"A small web server might use an Amazon Linux AMI, a general-purpose instance, an EBS volume, and a security group allowing HTTPS.","terms":[["EC2 instance","A virtual server running in AWS."],["AMI","A template used to launch EC2 instances."],["Instance type","A hardware profile defining compute resources."]],"check":{"q":"What does an EC2 instance type mainly define?","options":["CPU, memory, and related capacity","The IAM password","The S3 object name","The Route 53 domain"],"correct":0,"explain":"Instance types are resource profiles for virtual servers."}},{"title":"Load balancers and Auto Scaling","duration":8,"lead":"A load balancer spreads traffic, while Auto Scaling adjusts the number of application instances.","plain":"Together, they help an application handle changing traffic and replace unhealthy servers. They are commonly used across multiple Availability Zones.","analogy":"A receptionist sends customers to available service desks, while a manager opens or closes desks based on demand.","flow":["Users","Load balancer","Healthy instances","Auto Scaling adds or removes instances"],"points":["An Application Load Balancer is commonly used for HTTP and HTTPS applications.","Health checks prevent traffic from going to unhealthy targets.","An Auto Scaling group maintains minimum, desired, and maximum capacity.","Scaling policies react to measurements such as CPU or request count."],"example":"During a sale, an Auto Scaling group launches more EC2 instances. The ALB begins sending traffic after they pass health checks.","terms":[["Load balancer","A service that distributes traffic among targets."],["Health check","A test used to decide whether a target can receive traffic."],["Auto Scaling group","A group that maintains and adjusts EC2 capacity."]],"check":{"q":"Which component decides which healthy server receives an HTTP request?","options":["Application Load Balancer","EBS snapshot","IAM group","KMS key"],"correct":0,"explain":"An ALB distributes HTTP or HTTPS requests among healthy targets."},"lab":"load-balancing.html"},{"title":"Lambda and serverless","duration":7,"lead":"AWS Lambda runs functions without you managing servers directly.","plain":"You provide code and configure triggers, memory, timeout, and permissions. AWS starts execution environments when events arrive and charges based on requests and execution.","analogy":"Instead of renting a kitchen all day, you pay a kitchen to prepare each order when it arrives.","flow":["Event arrives","Lambda starts function","Code runs","Result or next event"],"points":["Lambda is useful for short event-driven tasks and APIs.","Functions have time and resource limits.","Concurrency is the number of invocations running at the same time.","Cold starts can add startup latency for new execution environments."],"example":"Uploading an image to S3 can trigger a Lambda function that creates a thumbnail.","terms":[["Serverless","A model where the provider manages server infrastructure and scaling."],["Trigger","An event source that starts a function."],["Concurrency","The number of function executions running simultaneously."]],"check":{"q":"What can directly trigger a Lambda function?","options":["An event such as an S3 object upload","Only a human pressing a button","An EC2 operating system patch","A billing address"],"correct":0,"explain":"Lambda is commonly started by events from other AWS services."},"lab":"lambda-concurrency.html"}]},{"title":"Storage: files and disks","subtitle":"Separate object, block, and shared file storage.","lessons":[{"title":"S3 object storage","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon S3 stores objects such as images, documents, backups, and logs inside buckets.","plain":"S3 is not a normal disk attached to a server. Applications access objects through APIs using a bucket name and object key.","analogy":"S3 is like a huge labeled warehouse. Every item has an address, but you do not mount it as a normal computer disk.","flow":["Create bucket","Upload object","Set permissions","Read by key","Apply lifecycle rules"],"points":["Buckets have globally unique names within an AWS partition.","Objects contain data and metadata.","S3 is designed for very high durability.","Storage classes trade cost, access frequency, and retrieval behavior.","Versioning can keep earlier object versions."],"example":"Store user-uploaded photos in a private S3 bucket and let the application generate temporary signed URLs for access.","terms":[["Bucket","A top-level container for S3 objects."],["Object key","The name or path used to identify an object."],["Storage class","A cost and access tier for S3 objects."]],"check":{"q":"Which service is normally best for storing millions of uploaded photos?","options":["Amazon S3","An IAM group","A security group","Route 53 health check"],"correct":0,"explain":"S3 is scalable object storage designed for files and objects."},"lab":"s3-lifecycle.html"},{"title":"EBS block storage","duration":7,"lead":"Amazon EBS provides persistent virtual disks commonly attached to EC2 instances.","plain":"An operating system sees an EBS volume much like a disk. You format it with a file system and use it for boot volumes or application data.","analogy":"EBS is like a removable hard drive assigned to a virtual server.","flow":["Create volume in an AZ","Attach to EC2","Format and mount","Read and write blocks","Snapshot for backup"],"points":["An EBS volume exists in one Availability Zone.","It can persist after an EC2 instance stops or terminates, depending on settings.","Snapshots are backups stored by AWS and can create new volumes.","Different volume types suit general, high-IOPS, or throughput workloads."],"example":"A database installed directly on EC2 may store its data files on an EBS volume.","terms":[["Block storage","Storage presented as blocks to an operating system, similar to a disk."],["Volume","An EBS virtual disk."],["Snapshot","A point-in-time backup of an EBS volume."]],"check":{"q":"Which storage looks most like a disk attached to an EC2 instance?","options":["Amazon EBS","Amazon Route 53","AWS IAM","Amazon SNS"],"correct":0,"explain":"EBS provides block volumes that an EC2 operating system can format and mount."}},{"title":"EFS shared file storage","duration":6,"lead":"Amazon EFS provides a managed shared file system for Linux workloads.","plain":"Multiple EC2 instances can mount the same EFS file system, including instances in different Availability Zones in the same Region.","analogy":"EFS is like a shared network drive that many office computers can open at the same time.","flow":["Create file system","Create mount targets","Mount from instances","Share the same files"],"points":["EFS uses the NFS protocol.","Capacity grows and shrinks automatically.","It is useful when multiple Linux servers require the same files.","It is not the same as S3 object storage or one-instance EBS storage."],"example":"A fleet of web servers can use EFS for shared user-uploaded files when the application expects a normal file system.","terms":[["File storage","Storage organized as directories and files."],["NFS","A network file-sharing protocol commonly used by Linux systems."],["Mount target","A network access point for an EFS file system in a VPC."]],"check":{"q":"Which service lets several Linux EC2 instances mount the same managed file system?","options":["Amazon EFS","Amazon EBS only","IAM Identity Center","AWS Budgets"],"correct":0,"explain":"EFS is managed shared NFS file storage."},"lab":"storage-selector.html"}]},{"title":"Networking: VPC from zero","subtitle":"Understand private networks, subnets, routes, and firewalls.","lessons":[{"title":"VPCs and subnets","duration":9,"lead":"A VPC is your private network inside AWS. Subnets divide that network into smaller address ranges.","plain":"When you launch resources, you choose a VPC and usually a subnet. A subnet belongs to one Availability Zone.","analogy":"A VPC is a company campus. Subnets are separate buildings or floors, each with its own address range and routing choices.","flow":["Choose VPC CIDR","Create subnets","Assign each subnet to an AZ","Place resources","Configure routes"],"points":["CIDR notation describes an IP address range.","A subnet cannot span multiple Availability Zones.","Public and private are design descriptions based mainly on routing—not special subnet types.","Route tables decide where network traffic can go."],"example":"Create public subnets for load balancers and private subnets for application and database resources.","terms":[["VPC","A logically isolated virtual network in AWS."],["Subnet","A portion of a VPC IP range located in one AZ."],["Route table","Rules that direct network traffic to destinations."]],"check":{"q":"Can one subnet span two Availability Zones?","options":["No, a subnet belongs to one AZ","Yes, every subnet spans a Region","Only an S3 subnet can","Only if it is public"],"correct":0,"explain":"Each subnet is created in exactly one Availability Zone."},"lab":"vpc.html"},{"title":"Public and private internet access","duration":9,"lead":"A public subnet can route to an internet gateway. Private resources often use a NAT gateway for outbound IPv4 access.","plain":"An internet gateway connects a VPC to the internet. A public resource also needs a public IP and suitable security rules. A NAT gateway lets private instances start outbound connections without accepting unsolicited inbound internet connections.","analogy":"A public subnet has a street-facing door. A private subnet uses a controlled outgoing reception desk rather than giving every room a street door.","flow":["Public subnet → Internet gateway","Private subnet → NAT gateway","NAT gateway → Internet gateway","Return traffic follows connection"],"points":["An internet gateway is attached to a VPC.","A NAT gateway normally sits in a public subnet.","Private subnet route tables send internet-bound IPv4 traffic to the NAT gateway.","VPC endpoints can provide private access to supported AWS services without NAT or internet routing."],"example":"Private EC2 instances download software updates through a NAT gateway while remaining unreachable directly from the internet.","terms":[["Internet gateway","A VPC component that enables internet routing."],["NAT gateway","A managed service used mainly for outbound IPv4 access from private subnets."],["VPC endpoint","Private connectivity from a VPC to supported services."]],"check":{"q":"What commonly gives private EC2 instances outbound IPv4 internet access?","options":["A NAT gateway in a public subnet","A larger EBS volume","An IAM group","An S3 lifecycle rule"],"correct":0,"explain":"Private subnet routes commonly send outbound internet traffic through a NAT gateway."},"lab":"vpc.html"},{"title":"Security groups and network ACLs","duration":8,"lead":"Security groups protect resources. Network ACLs provide an additional subnet-level control.","plain":"Security groups are stateful: return traffic for an allowed connection is automatically allowed. Network ACLs are stateless: inbound and outbound rules are evaluated separately.","analogy":"A security group is a guard assigned to a specific office. A network ACL is a checkpoint at the entrance to the whole floor.","flow":["Packet enters subnet","Network ACL checked","Security group checked","Application receives traffic"],"points":["Security groups are attached to network interfaces and resources.","Security groups contain allow rules, not explicit deny rules.","Network ACLs support allow and deny rules.","Use security-group references to allow traffic between application tiers."],"example":"Allow an ALB security group to reach the application security group on port 443, without allowing every internet address directly.","terms":[["Security group","A stateful virtual firewall attached to resources."],["Network ACL","A stateless subnet-level network filter."],["Stateful","Return traffic is automatically recognized for an allowed connection."]],"check":{"q":"Which control is stateful and normally attached to an EC2 network interface?","options":["Security group","Network ACL","Route 53 record","S3 bucket name"],"correct":0,"explain":"Security groups are stateful resource-level virtual firewalls."}}]},{"title":"Databases and caching","subtitle":"Choose relational, NoSQL, and cache services by need.","lessons":[{"title":"RDS and Aurora","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon RDS runs managed relational databases such as PostgreSQL and MySQL. Aurora is an AWS-built relational engine compatible with MySQL or PostgreSQL.","plain":"Relational databases store data in tables with defined relationships and support SQL. AWS manages many operational tasks such as backups, maintenance, and replacement of underlying infrastructure.","analogy":"A relational database is like a set of carefully linked spreadsheets with strong rules that keep related records consistent.","flow":["Application sends SQL","Database checks and changes rows","Transaction completes","Results returned"],"points":["Use relational databases when relationships, transactions, and SQL are important.","Multi-AZ deployments improve availability.","Read replicas help scale read traffic.","A standby for Multi-AZ availability is not the same as a read replica."],"example":"An online store may use RDS or Aurora for customers, orders, payments, and inventory relationships.","terms":[["Relational database","A database organized into related tables."],["SQL","A language used to query and change relational data."],["Transaction","A group of changes that succeeds or fails as one unit."]],"check":{"q":"Which service is a managed relational database option?","options":["Amazon RDS","Amazon SQS","Amazon CloudFront","AWS IAM"],"correct":0,"explain":"RDS provides managed relational database engines."}},{"title":"DynamoDB NoSQL basics","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless key-value and document database designed for low-latency access at large scale.","plain":"Instead of joining many related tables, DynamoDB is designed around known access patterns. The partition key determines how items are distributed and accessed.","analogy":"DynamoDB is like an enormous wall of labeled lockers. If you know the correct key label, you can reach an item very quickly.","flow":["Choose access pattern","Design partition key","Write items","Read by key or index","Scale automatically"],"points":["A good partition key spreads traffic across many values.","Hot partition keys can concentrate traffic and reduce performance.","DynamoDB supports optional sort keys and secondary indexes.","It is not simply a drop-in replacement for every relational database."],"example":"A game can store player state with playerId as a partition key and retrieve one player's record quickly.","terms":[["NoSQL","A broad group of non-relational database models."],["Partition key","The main key used to distribute and locate DynamoDB items."],["Item","A single DynamoDB record."]],"check":{"q":"Why is a partition key with many well-distributed values useful?","options":["It spreads data and traffic","It makes every table relational","It creates an internet gateway","It disables permissions"],"correct":0,"explain":"Well-distributed keys reduce the chance that one partition receives too much traffic."},"lab":"dynamodb.html"},{"title":"Caching and choosing a database","duration":7,"lead":"A cache stores frequently used data in faster temporary storage to reduce latency and database load.","plain":"Amazon ElastiCache provides managed Redis or Memcached. A cache is usually placed in front of a database, not used as the only durable source of truth.","analogy":"A kitchen keeps common ingredients on the counter instead of walking to the warehouse for every order.","flow":["Application checks cache","Cache hit returns quickly","Cache miss reads database","Result placed in cache"],"points":["RDS and Aurora fit relational data and transactions.","DynamoDB fits key-based access and very large scale.","ElastiCache accelerates repeated access but cached data may expire.","Start from data relationships, access patterns, consistency, scale, and operations."],"example":"Cache a product catalog in Redis while keeping the durable product records in Aurora.","terms":[["Cache","Fast temporary storage for frequently used data."],["Cache hit","The requested data is found in the cache."],["Cache miss","The application must retrieve data from the original source."]],"check":{"q":"What is the usual purpose of ElastiCache?","options":["Reduce latency and database load","Replace every durable database","Create IAM users","Store EBS snapshots"],"correct":0,"explain":"A cache keeps frequently accessed data closer to the application for faster reads."},"lab":"database-selector.html"}]},{"title":"Availability and recovery","subtitle":"Keep services working and recover from failures.","lessons":[{"title":"Designing for failure","duration":8,"lead":"Cloud architecture assumes components can fail and avoids depending on one irreplaceable resource.","plain":"Use multiple Availability Zones, health checks, automatic replacement, and managed services that support failover.","analogy":"A store with one cashier stops when that cashier leaves. A store with several trained cashiers can continue.","flow":["Component fails","Health check detects","Traffic moves","Replacement starts","Service continues"],"points":["Avoid a single point of failure.","Use at least two AZs for important regional applications.","Automate replacement instead of relying only on manual action.","Test failure behavior before an emergency."],"example":"An ALB sends traffic only to healthy EC2 instances across two AZs while Auto Scaling replaces failed instances.","terms":[["Single point of failure","One component whose failure stops the whole service."],["Failover","Moving work to a healthy component or location."],["Redundancy","Having additional components that can take over."]],"check":{"q":"What is a single point of failure?","options":["One component that can stop the whole system if it fails","A service running in two AZs","A backup copy","A health check"],"correct":0,"explain":"A single point of failure has no alternative component to continue its job."},"lab":"autoscaling.html"},{"title":"Backups, snapshots, and replication","duration":8,"lead":"Backups preserve recoverable copies. Replication keeps additional copies closer to current data.","plain":"Backups help recover from deletion, corruption, or disaster. Replication can improve availability, but a bad change may also replicate, so it does not replace backups.","analogy":"A spare live document helps if one laptop fails; an older protected copy helps if someone deletes the wrong paragraphs everywhere.","flow":["Production data","Backup schedule","Protected copy","Restore test","Recovery"],"points":["Define how much data loss is acceptable.","Define how long recovery may take.","Store backups independently and protect them from accidental deletion.","Regularly test restoring data."],"example":"Take automated RDS backups and keep cross-Region copies for a critical database according to recovery requirements.","terms":[["Backup","A recoverable copy of data."],["Replication","Maintaining additional copies of data, often continuously."],["Restore","Creating usable data or a resource from a backup."]],"check":{"q":"Why does replication not completely replace backups?","options":["Bad changes or deletions may also replicate","Replication never copies data","Backups are only for billing","Replication is an IAM policy"],"correct":0,"explain":"Replication improves availability, but backups preserve recoverable points in time."}},{"title":"Disaster recovery strategies","duration":9,"lead":"Disaster recovery choices balance recovery speed, acceptable data loss, cost, and complexity.","plain":"Common strategies range from backup and restore to pilot light, warm standby, and active/active multi-site designs.","analogy":"You can keep building plans in storage, keep a small emergency office ready, keep a reduced working office, or operate two full offices at once.","flow":["Set RTO and RPO","Choose strategy","Prepare data and infrastructure","Test failover","Improve plan"],"points":["RTO is the target time to restore service.","RPO is the acceptable amount of data loss measured in time.","Faster recovery normally costs more.","A strategy should match business requirements rather than simply choosing the most complex option."],"example":"A noncritical internal system may use backup and restore, while a revenue system may use warm standby in another Region.","terms":[["RTO","Recovery Time Objective: how quickly service should return."],["RPO","Recovery Point Objective: how much recent data loss is acceptable."],["Warm standby","A smaller but functional environment ready to scale during recovery."]],"check":{"q":"Which objective describes acceptable data loss measured in time?","options":["RPO","RTO","CPU","TTL only"],"correct":0,"explain":"RPO describes how far back in time a recovery point may be."},"lab":"disaster-recovery.html"}]},{"title":"Messaging and serverless integration","subtitle":"Decouple parts of an application with queues and events.","lessons":[{"title":"SQS queues","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon SQS stores messages until consumers are ready to process them.","plain":"A producer sends a message to a queue. A consumer receives and processes it, then deletes it. The queue helps the two sides operate at different speeds.","analogy":"A restaurant order board holds tickets even if the kitchen is busy. Orders wait safely instead of being lost.","flow":["Producer sends","Queue stores","Consumer receives","Visibility timeout","Consumer deletes"],"points":["SQS decouples producer and consumer availability.","The visibility timeout hides a message while a consumer works on it.","A message can return if processing fails or deletion does not occur.","A dead-letter queue isolates repeatedly failing messages."],"example":"An order API puts jobs into SQS so background workers can process them without delaying the customer request.","terms":[["Producer","A component that sends messages."],["Consumer","A component that receives and processes messages."],["Visibility timeout","The period a received message is temporarily hidden from other consumers."]],"check":{"q":"Why place SQS between an API and background workers?","options":["To buffer work and decouple the components","To create a VPC CIDR","To store EC2 boot disks","To replace DNS"],"correct":0,"explain":"SQS lets producers and consumers operate independently and at different rates."},"lab":"sqs.html"},{"title":"SNS and EventBridge","duration":8,"lead":"SNS broadcasts messages to subscribers. EventBridge routes events based on rules.","plain":"Use SNS when one published message should be pushed to multiple subscribers. Use EventBridge when events need rule-based routing across applications and AWS services.","analogy":"SNS is a loudspeaker announcement to subscribed rooms. EventBridge is a mail-sorting center that reads labels and routes each item to the correct destination.","flow":["Event or message","Topic or event bus","Subscriptions or rules","One or many targets"],"points":["SNS supports fan-out to endpoints such as SQS queues, Lambda, and notifications.","EventBridge uses event patterns to match and route events.","SQS stores messages for consumers; SNS primarily pushes to subscribers.","Services are often combined, such as SNS sending to multiple SQS queues."],"example":"An order-created event can trigger billing, analytics, and email workflows independently.","terms":[["Topic","An SNS communication channel."],["Event bus","An EventBridge channel that receives events."],["Fan-out","Delivering one message or event to multiple consumers."]],"check":{"q":"Which pattern sends one event to several independent consumers?","options":["Fan-out","One EBS volume","A single root user","A private IP only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Fan-out distributes one message or event to multiple subscribers or targets."}},{"title":"API Gateway and Step Functions","duration":7,"lead":"API Gateway provides managed API entry points. Step Functions coordinates multi-step workflows.","plain":"API Gateway receives HTTP requests and can call Lambda or other backends. Step Functions manages a sequence of tasks, choices, retries, and failures.","analogy":"API Gateway is the reception desk. Step Functions is the process checklist that ensures each department completes its step in order.","flow":["Client calls API","API Gateway validates/routes","Lambda or service runs","Workflow coordinates steps","Response returned"],"points":["API Gateway can handle authentication, throttling, and request routing.","Step Functions represents workflow state explicitly.","Retries and error paths are easier to understand than custom coordination code.","Not every simple operation needs a workflow service."],"example":"A document-processing API uploads a file, starts a Step Functions workflow, scans it, extracts text, and records the result.","terms":[["API","A defined way for software systems to communicate."],["Workflow","A sequence of coordinated tasks and decisions."],["State machine","The Step Functions definition of workflow states and transitions."]],"check":{"q":"Which service is designed to coordinate several Lambda functions with retries and choices?","options":["AWS Step Functions","Amazon EBS","Route 53 Resolver only","IAM group"],"correct":0,"explain":"Step Functions manages multi-step workflows, branching, retries, and error handling."}}]},{"title":"Monitoring and security services","subtitle":"Observe activity, protect data, and respond to problems.","lessons":[{"title":"CloudWatch metrics, logs, and alarms","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon CloudWatch collects operational data and can alert or act when conditions are met.","plain":"Metrics are numeric measurements, logs are text records, and alarms evaluate metrics over time.","analogy":"Metrics are dashboard gauges, logs are the written diary, and alarms are warning lights.","flow":["Resource produces data","CloudWatch stores metric or log","Alarm evaluates","Notification or action"],"points":["Common EC2 metrics include CPU utilization and network activity.","Applications can publish custom metrics and logs.","An alarm evaluates one or more periods against a threshold.","A dashboard shows selected operational information."],"example":"Create an alarm that sends a notification when an application's error count remains high for several minutes.","terms":[["Metric","A numerical measurement over time."],["Log","A timestamped record of events or messages."],["Alarm","A rule that watches a metric and changes state when conditions are met."]],"check":{"q":"Which CloudWatch feature evaluates a metric against a threshold?","options":["Alarm","Bucket","Subnet","Role session"],"correct":0,"explain":"CloudWatch alarms evaluate metric data and can notify or trigger actions."},"lab":"cloudwatch.html"},{"title":"CloudTrail and Config","duration":7,"lead":"CloudTrail records many AWS API activities. AWS Config records resource configuration and evaluates rules.","plain":"CloudTrail helps answer who performed an action and when. Config helps answer how resources are configured and whether they follow desired rules.","analogy":"CloudTrail is an activity logbook. Config is an inventory plus a compliance inspector.","flow":["API action","CloudTrail event","Resource configuration change","Config history/rule"],"points":["CloudTrail events support auditing and investigations.","Config can track configuration changes over time.","Config rules can identify noncompliant resources.","These services complement CloudWatch rather than replacing it."],"example":"Use CloudTrail to identify who changed a security group and Config to see how the group configuration changed.","terms":[["Audit trail","A record of actions used for accountability and investigation."],["Configuration history","A record of how a resource's settings changed."],["Compliance rule","A check that compares configuration with a desired requirement."]],"check":{"q":"Which service helps identify who called an AWS API?","options":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon EFS","AWS Auto Scaling","Amazon CloudFront"],"correct":0,"explain":"CloudTrail records many account and service API events."}},{"title":"Encryption, secrets, and edge protection","duration":8,"lead":"AWS provides services to protect data, credentials, and public applications.","plain":"KMS manages encryption keys, Secrets Manager stores and rotates secrets, WAF filters web requests, and Shield helps protect against distributed denial-of-service attacks.","analogy":"KMS manages locked safes and keys, Secrets Manager stores sensitive codes, WAF is a security guard checking visitors, and Shield absorbs disruptive crowds.","flow":["Encrypt data with KMS","Store credentials as secrets","Filter web requests with WAF","Protect availability with Shield"],"points":["Encryption at rest protects stored data.","Encryption in transit commonly uses TLS.","Do not hard-code passwords or API secrets in application code.","WAF rules can block patterns such as malicious IPs or request signatures."],"example":"Store a database password in Secrets Manager, encrypt the database with KMS, and attach WAF to a public CloudFront distribution or ALB.","terms":[["KMS","AWS Key Management Service for creating and controlling encryption keys."],["Secret","Sensitive data such as a password, token, or API credential."],["WAF","A web application firewall that evaluates HTTP requests."]],"check":{"q":"Where should an application store a database password instead of source code?","options":["AWS Secrets Manager","An open S3 website","A public Git repository","A Route 53 record"],"correct":0,"explain":"Secrets Manager is designed to store and manage sensitive application credentials."}}]},{"title":"Global delivery and performance","subtitle":"Deliver content quickly and route users safely.","lessons":[{"title":"Route 53 DNS","duration":7,"lead":"Amazon Route 53 provides DNS, domain registration, and health-check-based routing features.","plain":"DNS directs a name such as app.example.com to a resource. Route 53 offers routing policies for simple, weighted, latency, failover, and other patterns.","analogy":"DNS is the address book that tells a visitor where a named destination is located.","flow":["User enters name","DNS query","Route 53 returns record","Client connects to resource"],"points":["Alias records can point to selected AWS resources.","Health checks can support failover routing.","DNS answers may be cached according to TTL.","DNS routing does not move application data itself."],"example":"Route example.com to an Application Load Balancer using an alias record.","terms":[["DNS record","A mapping or instruction stored in DNS."],["Alias record","A Route 53 record that points to selected AWS resources."],["TTL","How long a DNS answer may be cached."]],"check":{"q":"What is Route 53 primarily used for in a web architecture?","options":["DNS and traffic routing decisions","Block storage","Running operating systems","Storing passwords"],"correct":0,"explain":"Route 53 is AWS's managed DNS and routing service."},"lab":"route53-delivery.html"},{"title":"CloudFront and caching","duration":8,"lead":"Amazon CloudFront caches and delivers content through edge locations closer to users.","plain":"CloudFront receives a user request. If the content is cached at the edge, it returns it quickly. Otherwise it retrieves the content from the origin and may cache it.","analogy":"Instead of every customer traveling to one central warehouse, local distribution centers keep popular products nearby.","flow":["User requests content","Nearest edge checks cache","Cache hit returns","Cache miss contacts origin","Response cached"],"points":["CloudFront can use S3, ALB, EC2, API Gateway, or other HTTP origins.","Caching reduces latency and origin load.","Cache keys and expiration affect correctness and performance.","CloudFront can integrate with TLS, WAF, and origin access controls."],"example":"Deliver images and JavaScript worldwide from an S3 origin using CloudFront.","terms":[["Edge location","A CloudFront site close to users that caches and delivers content."],["Origin","The source from which CloudFront retrieves content."],["Cache hit","A response served from the edge cache without contacting the origin."]],"check":{"q":"What is a major benefit of CloudFront?","options":["Lower latency and less origin traffic","It creates IAM users","It replaces all databases","It mounts EBS volumes"],"correct":0,"explain":"CloudFront serves cached content from locations closer to users."}},{"title":"Simple performance choices","duration":7,"lead":"Performance improves by reducing work, moving data closer, using the right storage or database, and scaling bottlenecks.","plain":"Measure before optimizing. A fast component does not help if another component is the real bottleneck.","analogy":"Making the kitchen faster does not shorten delivery if every order still waits at one checkout desk.","flow":["Measure latency","Find bottleneck","Choose change","Test result","Monitor again"],"points":["Cache repeated reads when data freshness allows.","Scale stateless compute horizontally.","Use a database access pattern that matches the workload.","Reduce unnecessary cross-Region or cross-AZ communication.","Use CloudWatch data to confirm improvement."],"example":"If a product page repeatedly reads the same database rows, add a cache and measure whether database load and response time improve.","terms":[["Latency","Time required to complete an operation or request."],["Throughput","Amount of work completed in a period of time."],["Bottleneck","The component that limits overall performance."]],"check":{"q":"What should you do before choosing a performance fix?","options":["Measure and identify the bottleneck","Add every AWS service","Move everything to one server","Disable logs"],"correct":0,"explain":"Measurements help ensure you optimize the component actually limiting performance."}}]},{"title":"Cost and architecture decisions","subtitle":"Build only what the requirement needs.","lessons":[{"title":"How AWS pricing works","duration":8,"lead":"AWS costs come from provisioned capacity, usage, storage, requests, data transfer, and service-specific charges.","plain":"There is no single pricing rule for all services. Learn the main billing dimension of each service and remove unused resources.","analogy":"A utility bill may include connection fees, usage, storage, and delivery charges. AWS services have different combinations of these.","flow":["Choose service","Understand billing unit","Estimate usage","Set budget","Measure actual cost"],"points":["On-Demand compute is flexible with no long commitment.","Savings Plans or Reserved Instances exchange commitment for discounts.","Spot Instances are discounted but can be interrupted.","Data transfer and NAT processing can be important costs.","Stopped resources may still have storage or other charges."],"example":"A temporary test server can use On-Demand pricing and be terminated after use; a steady production workload may benefit from a commitment discount.","terms":[["On-Demand","Pay for eligible compute without a long-term usage commitment."],["Spot","Discounted spare EC2 capacity that may be interrupted."],["Savings Plan","A commitment-based discount model for eligible compute usage."]],"check":{"q":"Which purchase option suits interruption-tolerant batch jobs seeking low EC2 cost?","options":["Spot Instances","Dedicated Hosts only","A larger NAT gateway","An IAM group"],"correct":0,"explain":"Spot capacity is discounted because AWS may interrupt it."},"lab":"cost-optimizer.html"},{"title":"Budgets, tags, and cost habits","duration":7,"lead":"Good cost control begins with visibility, ownership, and automatic alerts.","plain":"Use tags to identify resources, Budgets to alert on planned limits, and Cost Explorer to understand spending patterns.","analogy":"A household controls spending by labeling expenses, setting monthly limits, and reviewing statements.","flow":["Tag resources","Create budget","Watch cost trends","Find waste","Remove or resize"],"points":["Tag resources by project, environment, owner, or cost center.","Create billing alerts before experimenting.","Delete unused load balancers, NAT gateways, volumes, and snapshots when appropriate.","Rightsize resources using actual utilization.","Do not optimize cost by removing required security or resilience."],"example":"Set a monthly budget alert and tag all lab resources with Project=aws-study so they are easy to locate and remove.","terms":[["Tag","A key-value label attached to an AWS resource."],["AWS Budgets","A service that tracks cost or usage against thresholds."],["Rightsizing","Matching resource capacity to actual workload needs."]],"check":{"q":"What helps group costs by project?","options":["Consistent resource tags","One shared root password","A public subnet","A longer DNS TTL only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Tags can be activated for cost allocation and used to organize spending."}},{"title":"A beginner architecture checklist","duration":10,"lead":"Use a repeatable checklist instead of choosing services from memory.","plain":"For every design, identify users, traffic, data, failure requirements, security needs, operations, and cost limits. Then choose the simplest services that meet those requirements.","analogy":"An architect asks who will use a building, how many people it holds, what must stay secure, and what happens during an emergency before choosing materials.","flow":["Understand requirements","Draw request flow","Choose data stores","Remove single failures","Secure access","Monitor and estimate cost"],"points":["Who are the users and where are they located?","What protocols and traffic volume are expected?","What data must persist, and how is it accessed?","What RTO and RPO are required?","Who needs access, and how is data encrypted?","How will the system scale, fail, recover, and be monitored?","What is the simplest design that satisfies all requirements?"],"example":"For a small photo app: Route 53 → CloudFront → ALB → Auto Scaling EC2; S3 for photos; RDS for user records; private subnets for app and database; CloudWatch for monitoring.","terms":[["Requirement","A condition the design must satisfy."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or disadvantage."],["Architecture","The structure and interaction of system components."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide an AWS service choice?","options":["The application's requirements and tradeoffs","The longest service name","The largest possible instance","Using every managed service"],"correct":0,"explain":"Architecture choices should directly satisfy stated requirements with appropriate tradeoffs."},"lab":"storage-selector.html"}]}];
